The number of moles of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ in $45 \mathrm{~mL}$ of $0.10 \mathrm{M} \mathrm{Hg}\left(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\right)_{2}$ solution is
\begin{align*} \label{eq:1} \text{moles of } \mathrm{Hg}^{2+} &= \text{concentration} \times \text{volume} \\ &= 0.10 \mathrm{M} \times 45 \times 10^{-3} \mathrm{~L} \\ &= 0.045 \mathrm{~mol} \end{align*}
According to the reaction, $2 \mathrm{NaI}(\mathrm{aq})+\mathrm{Hg}\left(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\right)_{2}(\mathrm{aq}) \rightarrow \mathrm{HgI}_{2}(\mathrm{~s})+2 \mathrm{NaNO}_{3}(\mathrm{aq})$, $2$ moles of $\mathrm{NaI}$ are required to precipitate $1$ mole of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$.
Therefore, the number of moles of $\mathrm{NaI}$ needed to precipitate $0.045 \mathrm{~mol}$ of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ is
\begin{align*} \text{moles of } \mathrm{NaI} &= \frac{2}{1} \times \text{moles of } \mathrm{Hg}^{2+} \\ &= 2 \times 0.045 \mathrm{~mol} \\ &= 0.090 \mathrm{~mol} \end{align*}
